    files=(
        CatalogFile(
            path='SKILL.md',
            content='---\nname: Run Postmortem Audit\ndescription: Audits whether a finished agentic run actually works — build gate, stub sweep, call-site symmetry, signal checks, end-to-end smoke, verdict report, fix cards. Use after a run completes for a postmortem or done-vs-working acceptance audit.\ntoolsets: [start-here, work-management, knowledge-content, autonomous-operations]\n---\n\n# Run Postmortem Audit\n\nA board can land every card Done — each merged, unit-green, technically\ntrue card-by-card — while the product **cannot perform its north-star\nfunction at all**: fakes wired at production seams, real implementations\ncalled only by tests, detection signals pumped nowhere, a cross-card UI\ntrap, a binary never built. This skill is the systematic audit that\ncatches that class after a run ends, and converts findings into\ncorrectly-filed fix work instead of prose.\n\nIt runs **after** the run: an in-run acceptance milestone card may have\ncovered build+smoke as part of the run itself; the postmortem is the\nindependent check that the milestone\'s claims hold — and the safety net\nwhen no milestone card existed.\n\n**Covers:** the build gate, the mechanical no-fake sweep, call-site\nsymmetry, signal-pump heuristics, the end-to-end smoke of the north-star\nflow, the structured verdict report, and filing fix cards with the\nrouting that makes them actionable.\n\n**Does not cover:** live-run monitoring, wedge diagnosis, or kill-vs-wait\ndecisions on a running loop; fixing what you find (file cards — a small\nunblock-everything patch may be *proposed*, never silently applied);\ngeneric code review — quality of merged code is the reviewer stage\'s\njob; this audit asks only "does the product work".\n\n## 1. Inputs\n\n- **Repo + integration head** — audit the integrated result, never a\n  stale PR branch. Resolve which repo and branch from the board\'s git\n  repos (in `get_project_context`): the bound repo\'s integration branch,\n  or its default branch when no integration branch is set; if multiple\n  repos qualify, ask.\n- **The board** — `get_project_context` for the definition, cards, and\n  notes; the audit cross-references findings against cards.\n- **The north-star function** — what the product must demonstrably do,\n  from the board definition (`objectives`/`scope`) or the committed plan\n  doc. If neither states it, ask — the smoke (step 6) is meaningless\n  without it.\n- **The build command** — from the repo\'s own docs (README/Makefile/CI),\n  never guessed.\n- **The run\'s own claims** — the milestone card\'s body/evidence, verdict\n  notes, any committed run reports. For per-card claims, read the review\n  verdict directly: `get_card_verdict(workspace_slug, board_id, card_id)`\n  returns the latest reviewer decision and its reasoning (404 = the card\n  was never reviewed — itself a lead). Read claims as claims to verify,\n  not as evidence.\n- **What actually merged** — `list_merge_queue` before trusting Done\n  columns: successfully merged entries drop out, so ANY surviving entry\n  (`queued | merging | conflict | failed`) is a card whose "Done" never\n  landed on the integration head you audit — check this before building\n  in step 2.\n- **The run history** — `list_executions(workspace_slug, card_id=…)` is a\n  card\'s pipeline trail, capped at the newest 200 rows (roles, decisions,\n  per-stage `cost_usd`/`tokens_used`): the money-loop/cost audit — a\n  runaway loop card can exceed the cap and under-count — and the answer\n  to "how did this card actually reach Done". The card scope ignores only\n  `limit` (it returns the card\'s full trail, newest 200 rows);\n  `status`/`role`/`agent_id` still filter within that window. Rows are\n  heavy (full prompts + tool invocations), so keep `limit` small on\n  unscoped reads.\n  `list_approvals(status="pending")` surfaces approval gates the run\n  ended without ever deciding — an undecided gate behind a Done card is a\n  finding lead.\n\nSession start: `get_server_info` reports the server version and the tools\nenabled in this session — a tool that "doesn\'t exist" is usually version\ndrift or an allowlist restriction, not a reason to fall back to manual\ndigging.\n\nWork read-only on the product code throughout (the only write is step\n8\'s report); evidence is file:line, grep output, and screenshots.\n\n## 2. The build gate\n\nRun the project\'s real build command first. A failed build or an **empty\nartifact** (the app bundle/binary missing where the platform expects it)\nis an automatic top-severity finding — runs have merged dozens of PRs\nwith the artifact directory empty because no card\'s DoD ever forced a\nbuild. Don\'t proceed to "does it work" before "does it exist".\n\n## 3. The no-fake sweep\n\nMechanical, cheap, catches the whole fake-left-in-prod class:\n\n```sh\ngrep -rni \'fake\\|silent\\|interim\\|stub\' <entrypoint> <wiring/DI layer> <constructor sites>\n```\n\n- Scope — discover it, don\'t guess: start from the build target\'s\n  entrypoint (main/cmd/app bootstrap), follow the import/construction\n  chain one hop to the wiring/DI module(s), and sweep those files\n  (typical homes: `cmd/`, `app/`, `di/`, `wiring.*`, the composition\n  root). If the wiring layer is unclear, sweep the whole repo and exclude\n  test/fixture paths. Not test code — fixtures legitimately contain\n  fakes.\n- What counts as a hit: the markers in **identifiers, types, or\n  constructor names** (the card-authoring convention names fakes with\n  exactly these markers so this sweep finds them), or comments marking a\n  stand-in at a seam. Incidental prose ("fails silently") is noted and\n  dismissed in one line, not filed. Case-insensitive, always; if the\n  board\'s authoring convention gains a marker, the sweep gains it too.\n- **Each hit is a finding unless it is tracked** — tracked means: an\n  open (not Done) card whose deliverable is replacing this fake, AND a\n  dependency edge making the card that shipped or consumes the fake\n  depend on that wiring card (check both with\n  `list_card_dependencies`). A card that merely mentions the fake, an\n  edge in the wrong direction, or a Done wiring card is not tracking. A\n  code comment ("swap when X lands") is never a tracker.\n\n## 4. Call-site symmetry\n\nThe inverse check: for every "real implementation" the run produced\n(public constructors of substantive packages), grep its call sites and\nrequire **at least one outside test files**:\n\n```sh\ngrep -rn \'NewRealThing(\' --include=\'*.go\' | grep -v \'_test.go\'\n```\n\n"Real impl exists, tests-only call sites" is **not done** at product\nlevel — it is the signature of a seam whose wiring card never existed.\nPair each orphaned impl with whatever stands in at its seam (usually a\nstep-3 hit) and file one finding for the pair.\n\n## 5. Signal-pump heuristics\n\nThe harder-to-grep cousins — wiring that exists but pumps nothing:\n\n- **Constructed-and-discarded**: an object built in the wiring layer\n  whose value is never stored, passed, or subscribed.\n- **Observers never fed**: `Observe`/`Handle`/`OnX` methods with zero\n  production callers — the event source exists, the sink exists, nobody\n  connects them.\n- **Channels/callbacks never read**: a producer writing to a channel no\n  prod goroutine reads; a callback registered nowhere.\n\nRead the wiring layer end to end (it is small) and trace each subsystem\'s\nsignal from source to consumer; anything that dead-ends is a `stubbed`\nfinding — the seam carries nothing real — even when both ends are\nindividually green.\n\n## 6. The end-to-end smoke\n\nThe only step that catches cross-card integration traps — two components\nindividually unit-green whose handoff is broken. Static checks cannot see\nthese; **never skip the smoke because steps 2–5 came back clean.**\n\n- Launch the **built** product (step 2\'s artifact) and execute the\n  north-star flow end to end, as a user would.\n- **Web UI** → drive the flow with browser automation, capturing\n  screenshots plus console/network errors as evidence.\n- **Desktop/webview app** → unless the app exposes a remote-debugging/\n  automation target, the packaged webview is not browser-testable;\n  execute the repo\'s committed smoke checklist manually on real hardware.\n  If no committed checklist exists, derive one from the north-star flow\n  and file its absence as a finding (process takeaway). Items needing\n  permissions/secrets/hardware you don\'t have are marked **unverified** —\n  unverified ≠ passing; if the whole smoke is unverifiable, say so in the\n  TL;DR and cap the audit verdict (you cannot conclude `works`).\n- Include the first-run path (onboarding, permissions) — first-run traps\n  are the classic cross-card bug.\n\n## 7. File the fix work — correctly, or the audit makes things worse\n\nFindings become cards; cards without edges become money-loops (the\nauditor re-audits the unfixed source forever while the fixes starve).\nThe filing rules, non-negotiable:\n\n1. Fix cards go to the **active-type column** — resolve it via\n   `get_board` by `column_type`, never by name; if no column carries the\n   type, ask — with labels `direct-implement` + `planned` (they skip\n   planning; in backlog they get re-planned or, with `planned`\n   pre-applied, dead-zone entirely), `card_type: bug`, and priority\n   mapped from severity: blocker → `urgent`, fix-soon → `high`,\n   polish → `low`.\n2. **Every fix card gets a dependency edge from each card it blocks**\n   (the blocked/audited card depends on the fix card —\n   `add_card_dependency` with the blocked card as the dependent). This is\n   the auditor invariant; it is what makes re-audits idle instead of\n   loops.\n3. Validate after filing: `validate_board_dependencies`, and re-read the\n   cards to confirm labels and edges landed (2xx ≠ persisted).\n4. **Self-check before finishing: if any filed fix card has no edge, the\n   audit is not done.** Add the missing edges, then finish.\n\nSeverity uses the actionability scale: `blocker` — the north-star\nfunction is impossible; `fix-soon` — works degraded or a trap on a\nprimary path; `polish` — cosmetic/deferred.\n\n## 8. The report\n\nStructure (full skeleton: `references/report-structure.md`):\n\n1. **TL;DR** — the one-paragraph verdict: can the product perform its\n   north-star function, and the count of blockers.\n2. **What actually works** — table of subsystems verified real, wired,\n   and exercised, each with evidence. The good news matters: it scopes\n   the fix work honestly.\n3. **The blocking gaps** — one section per gap: effect on the user,\n   mechanism (file:line), what it takes to fix.\n4. **Subsystem status matrix** — every component, one verdict each:\n   `works` (wired and exercised in prod) / `wired-but-broken` (real and\n   connected, fails in integration) / `stubbed` (a fake/stub stands in,\n   the real impl is never constructed in prod, or signals pump nowhere) /\n   `unbuilt` (artifact or step never produced) / `unverified` (could not\n   be exercised — absence of evidence, never counted as working), with\n   file:line.\n5. **What it takes to make it work** — the gaps ordered by dependency,\n   mapped to the filed fix cards.\n6. **Process takeaway** — which authoring rule would have prevented each\n   gap (feeds the next board\'s setup; usually: integration cards,\n   wire-or-file, the milestone card, the body marker).\n\nRoute the verdict: write the report to the repo or the project\'s reports\nconvention, and post a board note (`create_note`, kind `user_note`,\nboard-scoped, pinned if the board\'s convention pins verdicts) linking the\nreport and the filed cards.\n',
        ),
        CatalogFile(
            path='references/report-structure.md',
            content='# Done-vs-actually-working report structure\n\nModeled on the real audit that defined this skill\'s method. Keep the\nsection order — readers scan TL;DR → gaps → matrix.\n\n```markdown\n# <Project> — "Done" vs. Actually-Working Report\n\n**Audited against:** <branch> @ <sha>, board <name> (<N>/<N> cards Done)\n**Method:** <independent read-only subsystem audits + toolchain/runtime\nprobing; no code modified>\n\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n<One paragraph: is the card-by-card "Done" technically true? Can the\nproduct perform its north-star function end to end? The N hard blockers\nin one sentence each. What a user launching it today would experience.>\n\n---\n\n## What actually works\n\nReal, wired into the running app, and exercised — not stubs:\n\n| Subsystem | Status | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| <component> | Working | <file/test/probe evidence> |\n| <component> | Working (each part) — **but see Gap X: never fed** | <…> |\n\n**Toolchain on this machine:** <versions; build/test results; external\nbinaries present/absent>.\n\n---\n\n## The blocking gaps\n\n### Gap A — <one-line title>\n\n**Effect:** <what the user experiences because of this gap>.\n\n<Mechanism, numbered where compound: the exact construction site\n(file:line, quoted), the missing adapter/loop/resolution, what the code\ncomments self-document. Close with the fix shape: what it needs.>\n\n### Gap B — …\n### Gap <X> — <build/signing/packaging class>\n### Gap <Y> — cosmetic / deferred (non-blocking, for completeness)\n\n---\n\n## Subsystem status matrix\n\nLegend: works (wired + exercised in prod) · wired-but-broken ·\nstubbed or unbuilt · unverified (could not be exercised)\n\n| Area | Component | Verdict | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| <area> | <component> | stubbed — `NewFakeThing()` | <file:line> |\n| <area> | <component> | stubbed — tests-only call sites | <package> |\n\n---\n\n## What it takes to make it a working product\n\nOrdered by dependency; each item maps to a filed fix card.\n\n1. **<Gap A fix>** (card <id>, blocks <cards>) — <the work>; **verify:**\n   <the end-to-end assertion that proves it>.\n2. …\n\n---\n\n## Process takeaway (for the board / pipeline)\n\n<Which authoring rule would have prevented each gap: integration cards\nwith end-to-end DoD, the wire-or-file clause, the acceptance milestone\ncard, the UI-validation body marker. One paragraph; feeds the next run\'s\nsetup.>\n```\n\nVerdict definitions (use exactly these five):\n\n| verdict | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `works` | real implementation, wired into the running app, exercised by the smoke |\n| `wired-but-broken` | everything real and connected; fails in integration (cross-component handoff) |\n| `stubbed` | a fake/silent/interim/stub stands in at the prod seam, the real impl is never constructed in prod (tests-only call sites), or signals pump nowhere |\n| `unbuilt` | the artifact, model, or step was never produced/run |\n| `unverified` | could not be exercised (missing hardware/permissions/secrets) — absence of evidence; never counted as working |\n\nSeverity (actionability): `blocker` — the north-star function is\nimpossible; `fix-soon` — works degraded or a trap on a primary path;\n`polish` — cosmetic/deferred.\n\nBoard-side evidence sources (cite alongside file:line where a claim rests\non run history rather than code):\n\n- `get_card_verdict(workspace_slug, board_id, card_id)` — the reviewer\'s\n  approve/reject decision and reasoning for a card (404 = never reviewed).\n- `list_executions(workspace_slug, card_id=…)` — the card\'s pipeline\n  trail (newest 200 rows, `limit` ignored) with per-stage cost; other\n  filters (`status`/`role`/`agent_id`) still apply within that window.\n- `list_merge_queue(workspace_slug)` — surviving entries = work that\n  never landed on the integration head despite a Done column.\n',
        ),
    ),
